<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1876">1876</a>–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Quaid-e-Azam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad Ali Jinnah was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">All-India Muslim League</a> and the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. A barrister born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, he moved from Hindu-Muslim unity to demanding a separate Muslim homeland, set out in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore-resolution">Lahore Resolution</a> of 1940. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of Pakistan was created on 14 August 1947 he became its first Governor-General, and he died in office in September 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah.jpg" alt="Muhammad Ali Jinnah" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jinnah1945c.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jinnah was born Mahomedali Jinnahbhai, probably on 25 December 1876, at Wazir Mansion near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, then in the Bombay Presidency. His family came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and had followed the Nizari Isma'ili branch of Shia Islam; he later followed Twelver Shia teachings, and after his death relatives and other witnesses claimed that he had turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>. In 1892 a business associate of his father offered him an apprenticeship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. He accepted despite his mother's opposition, and before he left she had him marry a cousin, Emibai. Both women died while he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where he trained as a barrister at Lincoln's Inn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Jinnah began practice in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> at the age of 20, the only Muslim barrister in the city. His first three years brought few briefs, but he later earned large fees. He also took an interest in labour causes: he was elected president of the All India Postal Staff Union in 1925 and helped to pass the Trade Union Act of 1926. In politics he rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> and worked to bring Congress and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> together. As president of the League in 1916 he signed the Lucknow Pact, which set quotas for Muslim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> representation in the provinces, and Sarojini Naidu called him an ambassador of Hindu-Muslim unity.</p><p>In 1920 he left Congress, which had agreed to a campaign of satyagraha that he regarded as political anarchy. In 1923 he was elected Muslim member for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> in the Central Legislative Assembly, and in 1925 he declined a knighthood from Lord Reading, saying he preferred to be plain Mr Jinnah. In the early 1930s Muslims urged him to resume leadership of the inactive League. Muhammad Iqbal, with whom he corresponded in 1936 and 1937, urged Muslims to strengthen his hands, and Jinnah later called Iqbal his mentor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Until the late 1930s most Muslims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> expected to belong to a single state after independence. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> began, the British increasingly treated Jinnah and the League as the representatives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s Muslims, and the League passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore-resolution">Lahore Resolution</a> in 1940. Talks between Congress and the League failed to find a formula that would keep British India united, and the parties agreed instead to the independence of a Hindu-majority India and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>.</p><p>As first Governor-General, Jinnah worked to set up the new government and personally supervised refugee camps for the millions of Muslims who migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. He fixed his salary at 1 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupee</a> a month. He died on 11 September 1948, aged 71. He is revered in Pakistan as Quaid-e-Azam and Baba-e-Qaum, universities and public buildings bear his name, and his birthday is a national holiday.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad Ali Jinnah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sir Syed Ahmed Khan's two-nation theory led to the All-India Muslim League in 1906, and under <b>Muhammad Ali Jinnah</b> the Lahore Resolution of 1940 demanded a separate state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1930 the poet Muhammad Iqbal proposed a consolidated Muslim state in north-western India, and in the Lahore Resolution of March 1940 the League under <b>Muhammad Ali Jinnah</b> demanded “independent states” for the Muslim-majority areas, holding that Hindus and Muslims were two nations.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>The All-India Muslim League and <b>Muhammad Ali Jinnah</b> opposed the song entirely that year.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a></li><li><blockquote>On 14 August 1947 the Dominion of Pakistan came into being, with <b>Muhammad Ali Jinnah</b> as Governor-General and Karachi as capital, comprising West Pakistan and East Bengal.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-pakistan">Dominion of Pakistan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.86,67.01&amp;year=1922" aria-label="Karachi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6328 1249 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Muhammad Ali Jinnah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6778 1519h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6778 1519h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.86,67.01&amp;year=1922">What was here in 1922? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
